//! Port of services/api/src/mcp/guide.ts — what assistants are told about designing in Pinselo: short server
//! instructions plus the full guide tool.

const INSTRUCTIONS: &str = r#"{brand} is a design editor (graphics, presentations, documents, websites, video). These tools create and edit the user's designs. Before designing for a brand, call get_brand_kit and use the kit whose name matches the design system or brand the user selected or named (create the design with create_design { brand_kit }). If the kit lists guidelines, read them with get_brand_guidelines first and follow them throughout: they are the brand's own rules for copy, colour, type, layout, logos and imagery. Place its logo, and use its graphics and photos with add_image { asset } wherever the design has room for a picture. Never draw an imitation of a brand element (logo, underline, brush stroke, mascot, illustration) with text or shapes when the kit has the real one, and never invent quotes, people, prices or figures. If you use an image from outside the kit or leave one out, say so in your reply. An image the user gives you (attached to the chat, or a file you can read) goes into their uploads through upload_image or create_upload_link, then into the design with add_image { asset }; never leave it out silently. When the user asks you to set up their brand in {brand} (from a brand book, design system, website or files), make a kit with create_brand_kit, uploading its logos and images first, and change it later with update_brand_kit.
Workflow: find or create a design (list_designs, search_templates, create_design) → read it with get_design → change it with edit_design (many operations per call), whose check field lists layout problems it found (text that doesn't fit or runs off the page, poor contrast, overlaps) → fix those, then look at the result with screenshot_page. Read get_design_guide once before your first edit.
Coordinates are px at 96 dpi from the page's top-left; colours are hex. The user may have the design open: they watch your changes live, and can undo them all (undo_ai_changes, or "Undo AI changes" in the editor). Share the editUrl so they can open it."#;

const GUIDE: &str = r#"# Designing in {brand}

## Model
- A design has pages of one size (get_design → size). Every element has a page-space box x, y, w, h in px (96 dpi), measured from the page's top-left. Rotation is in degrees around the box centre.
- Elements are stacked bottom to top in the order get_design lists them; new elements go on top. Use reorder to change it.
- Types: text, shape, line, image, frame (a photo placeholder with a clip shape), group, table, chart, qr, video, embed.
- Locked elements ("locked": true) were locked by the user; you cannot move or change them.

## Editing (edit_design)
- Send all the changes for a step in one call; they apply in order as one change. Give new elements a "ref" and use "$ref" later in the same call (for example to group, arrange or align what you just added).
- Text: add_text with w (the wrap width). Leave h out so the box fits the text. Style with font, size (px), weight (100–900), color, align, lineHeight, letterSpacing, case. update with "text" replaces the words and keeps the style.
- Images: add_image takes { url } (public https), { stock: { provider, id } } from search_media, { asset } from list_uploads, upload_image or an upload link, or { brand: { kit, name } } for a file in a brand kit by its file name (the name from the design system works). fit "cover" (default) crops to fill the box.
- Files from the user: upload_image takes the attachment itself where the chat app passes attachments to tools (ChatGPT does: pass the attached file as file), or a very small file (up to 64 KB, such as an SVG logo) as data_base64, because base64 passes through your context. Otherwise create_upload_link: if you can run commands or make HTTP requests, send the file to the link yourself (curl -T <file> "<url>"; a file attached to the chat is usually at /mnt/user-data/uploads/<name>) and the reply names the asset. If you can't, give the user the link: they drop the file on that page, and get_upload_link tells you the asset. Offer this rather than leaving an image out, and if an image still couldn't be included, say so plainly.
- Layout: arrange stacks items in a row or column or on a grid, with gap, align, justify and stretch; within "page" lays them out inside the page minus padding. align and distribute line items up with the page or with each other.
- Depth: shadow { x, y, blur, color } on add_* (not add_line) or update gives an element a drop shadow; a soft one lifts a card off its background (y 8–16, blur 24–40, color #00000033, an 8-digit hex for transparency). blur (px) softens a whole element, such as a background photo behind text. null removes either.
- Pages: add_page (optionally duplicate an existing page), update_page (background, title, speaker notes, duration, transition), move_page, delete_page.
- Text everywhere: replace_text swaps every match of find for replace across the design (or one page, or given elements) and keeps each word's styling. Use it to fill in a template's placeholder text, rename a product, or translate (one replace_text per sentence).
- Other formats: copy_design makes a copy, optionally resized to another preset (an Instagram story from a post, a banner, A4). Elements are repositioned to fit; fix what its check reports. Copy first when translating, so the original stays.
- Many versions from data: bulk_create makes one copy per row (certificates, badges, a post per product). Put placeholder text in the design first (for example "{name}"), then pass rows like { "{name}": "Ada" }; elements bound to a column take the value for that column name. Its warning lists fields that matched nothing.
- Shapes: {shapes}.
- Charts: {charts}.

## Video and motion
- Sizes for social video: create_design { preset } with fb-reel or ig-reel (1080×1920, Reels and Stories), ig-video (1080×1350, feed) or video-square (1080×1080). These open as videos in the editor.
- Times are in seconds. Pages play one after another in videos, GIFs and auto-playing presentations: update_page { duration, transition: { type, duration } } sets a page's length (null: automatic, 5 s or its videos' length) and how it comes in from the page before (fade, slide, push, wipe, zoom, …; none cuts). An ad usually needs scenes of 1.5–3 s with short transitions (0.2–0.4 s). get_design's timeline lists page lengths, the soundtrack and captions.
- Elements: animation { in: { type, delay, duration, easing, distance }, out: { type, at, duration } } on any add_* or update. in.type: fade, rise, drop, slide-left/-right/-up/-down, scale, zoom, pop, wipe, draw (a line draws itself from its first point to its last), typewriter (text), blur; text can also animate by line, word or letter (by, stagger). delay counts from when the element appears; out.at is the page second the exit starts. visibleFrom / visibleTo show an element for part of a page, so one page can swap texts. animation.loop { type } keeps one thing moving after it has come in (pulse on a button, float on a sticker); use it on at most one element per scene. animation null removes all three.
- Photos that move: a still photo held for seconds makes a video look like a slideshow. Give every photo and background picture panZoom { type: zoom-in | zoom-out | pan-left | pan-right | pan-up | pan-down, amount, focus } on add_image, add_video or update: the picture moves slowly inside its box for the whole time it shows, and the box, its corners and its outline stay put. Keep amount small (0.08–0.2), set focus { x, y } on the subject (a face, the product) so the zoom closes in on it, and alternate directions from scene to scene (in, then out, then a pan) so cuts feel deliberate.
- Staggering: animate { ids, in, start, stagger } gives the listed elements the same entrance one after another (for example eyebrow, then headline, then button). update_page { entrance: { type, duration } } gives every element on a page without its own entrance the same one, in stacking order.
- Keyframes, for movement a preset can't give (a card that slides in, overshoots and settles; a logo that turns while it grows; a product that drifts across the scene): keyframes { position, scale, scaleX, scaleY, rotation, opacity, blur, anchor } on any add_* or update. Each is a list of keys { at, value, ease } in page seconds and in the same units as update: position is the [x, y] get_design shows for the element (page px; for a rotated element, the top-left of its bounds), rotation is degrees, opacity 0–1 (up to the element's own opacity), scale a multiple of its size, scaleX and scaleY multiply only its width or height (a bar that grows from 0, a card that turns like a page). anchor is the point scale and rotation pivot on, entrances included: top-left, top, top-right, left, center (the default), right, bottom-left, bottom, bottom-right, or [x, y] fractions of the box; with anchor bottom a bar grows up from its base, with anchor top a sign swings from its top edge. offset [dx, dy] is a move from where it rests, instead of position; x or y alone move one axis of an unrotated element. Before its first key an element holds the first value, after its last key the last one. ease shapes the move arriving at a key: ease (the default, slow at both ends), ease-out (arrives softly; best for things coming in), ease-in (leaves softly; for things going out), linear (steady; for drifts and turns), back (overshoots and settles), hold (jumps at the key); for more polish, out-quart or out-expo (arrive more softly than ease-out, the smooth stop of product videos), in-out-quart (a stronger ease), out-back (a tighter overshoot), anticipate (pulls back before it goes), or your own curve as [x1, y1, x2, y2], as in CSS cubic-bezier. Let the element rest at its final key where it is placed, so the layout get_design checks is the one people see. A track given replaces that track, null removes it, and keyframes: null removes them all. Keys play only while the element shows (visibleFrom / visibleTo), and they combine with its entrance, so key the whole move or use an entrance, not both on the same property.
- Keyframes that change how an element looks, in the same lists of keys: fill (a shape's colour; colours blend), strokeColor and strokeWidth (the outline of a shape or line, so an outline can draw on or pulse), radius (corners in px: a square that rounds into a circle), zoom (into an image or video inside its box: 1 as placed, 2 the middle half, [2, x, y] towards a point of the picture as fractions) and shadow ({ x, y, blur, color } per key: a card that lifts off the page as it rises, with y and blur growing). They set the value at each key instead of moving the element, and the element keeps its own value where it has no keys. motionBlur (0–1, the shutter: 0.5 is the usual film look) makes fast moves, spins and zooms streak along their path like footage; one key at 0 s on anything that moves fast is usually enough, as still and slow moments stay sharp. A gradient fill moves too: gradientAngle turns a linear gradient (a sheen sweeping across a button), and gradientColors keys list one colour per stop (a sky going from dawn to dusk).
- Paths: motionPath { via: [[x, y], …], duration, delay, easing, smooth } moves the element's top-left through page points (start at its resting place); smooth curves through them; delay counts from when the element appears.
- Groups: elements inside a group animate on their own (entrance, exit, loop, keyframes, motion path, visibleFrom / visibleTo), on top of whatever the group does, and a group's mask can move too. A mask makes a group show only through one of its own elements: group { ids, mask } or update { mask } (null removes it). With maskMode alpha (the default) the others show through the mask's shape and opacity, so text as the mask fills its letters with the photo behind; with luminance they show through its brightness, so a white-to-black gradient shape fades the group out. Animate the mask for reveals: a circle scaling up from 0 opens onto a photo, a bar sliding across wipes text in.
- Follow: follow: "<id>" on add_* or update makes an element take on another element's movement on the same page (its keyframes, motion path, entrance and loop moves, turns included), so a label rides a card in, a price tag swings with its sign, or a shadow stays under a bouncing logo, without keying both. At rest nothing changes, the follower keeps its own animation on top, and opacity is not passed on. null stops following.
- Reuse and timing: duplicate { ids, dx, dy, page, shift, refs } copies elements with their animation (groups with what is inside, masks and follows pointing at the copies), so one animated card becomes three, each shift seconds later. shift_time { ids, by } moves when elements show and all their keyframes later (or earlier, with a negative by), entrances, exits and loops with them: the easy way to retime a whole sequence.
- Camera: update_page { camera { zoom, look, rotation, shake, fixed } } films the whole page, background included, so a video feels shot rather than laid out: a slow push-in (zoom 1 to 1.15 over the page), a pan from one card to the next (look moves between page points, zoom 1.5 or more so there is room), a drifting turn of 1–3 degrees, or a short shake on an impact (amount 6–20 px for 0.2–0.5 s). zoom, look and rotation are keys { at, value, ease } like keyframes; zoom is raised on its own as far as needed so the page edges never show. fixed lists top-level elements it leaves in place, such as a logo or a price tag. Still exports show the page as it is. A page entrance applies to the page's top-level elements and groups, not to what is inside them. Moving an element inside a rotated or flipped group (position, offset, x, y, motionPath) is refused; it can still turn, scale and fade, or move the group instead.
- Brand motion: a kit with motion (get_brand_kit, get_design) sets the defaults: what an animation leaves out (type, duration, easing, distance; text and drawn lines separately), the stagger, the page transition, and bounce: false rules out pop. Leave those values out so the brand's own style applies; set motion on the kit with update_brand_kit when the user's brand book describes how it moves.
- Sound and video: add_audio adds music or a voice-over (an upload via list_uploads { type: "audio" }, search_media { type: "audio" }, or a URL); by default it runs to the end of the video and loops if shorter, with volume, fadeIn and fadeOut. Set page lengths first, or run update_audio { id, fit: true } after changing them. add_video places a clip (trim, muted, loop, fit, volume, speed: 0.5 for slow motion, up to 3); a page lasts as long as its videos unless its duration is set.
- Captions: most people watch social video without sound, so put the message on screen and caption any voice-over with set_captions { cues: [{ start, end, text }] } (video seconds). style.highlight (a colour) lights up the word being spoken, one after another; it reads best with short cues of 2–5 words in a large size.
- Export the video: export_design { format: "mp4" } returns a download link. A longer video answers { status: "rendering", export_id } first: call export_design again with the same arguments plus that export_id until the link comes back, and give the user the link; do not start a new export meanwhile.
- Check motion: screenshot_page { time } shows a page at one second, { frames: 6 } a filmstrip across it. edit_design's check reports text on screen too briefly to read (about 3 words a second plus half a second), text and buttons in the part of a 9:16 video that Reels and Stories cover (keep them between 14% and 65% of the height), a video too long for its format, and keyframes that cannot play as meant: after the page ends, outside the time the element shows, less than a frame apart, fighting its entrance, or keeping it off the page. After keyframing, check the moves with a filmstrip at the key times (screenshot_page { times }).
- Export: export_design { format: "mp4", pages } plays the pages given at the design's own size (scale or width to change it).

## Good design
- Start from the brand kit (get_brand_kit) whose name matches the design system or brand the user selected or named, and use only that kit's assets; if several could match, ask. Create the design with create_design { brand_kit } so it is made where the kit's fonts live and get_design keeps showing the kit. Its voice sums up how the brand writes: follow it for every word. A kit that lists guidelines has a full written brand book: read it with get_brand_guidelines before you design, and let it win over the general advice here. If the kit lists missingFonts, still use those fonts for their roles and tell the user to upload them. Use its colours, its fonts for each role, its textStyles (set every text's role, and its weight, italic, case, letterSpacing, lineHeight and colour as the matching style says; size is a reference for a typical page, scale it to the format; edit_design's check and get_design's styleCheck list text that strays), its logo, and its graphics and photos: each comes with an asset id and file name, so choose by name and check with screenshot_page (a logo that suits the background, for example). A design system in the chat gives you colours, fonts and rules, but its files can't reach {brand} from there, so use the kit's copies; if there is no kit for it yet and the user wants one, make it with create_brand_kit (upload the logos and images first with upload_image or create_upload_link and pass their asset ids). Prefer a real image to a type-only layout when the format has room. Never draw an imitation of a brand element (logo, underline, brush stroke, mascot, illustration) with text or shapes when the kit has the real one: look through its graphics first. Use only facts the user or the brand gave you: never invent quotes, people, prices or figures, and mark anything you must assume as a placeholder and say so. If the kit lacks something the design needs, finish with what's there and say exactly what's missing. When a team's kit governs the design, get_design shows it as brand (fonts by role, colours); if brand.enforced is set, edit_design only accepts those colours and fonts.
- Give elements a role (headline, subhead, body, cta, logo, hero-image, price, legal, …) when you add them, and use the brand font for that role. Roles keep the hierarchy clear for later edits and resizes.
- Prefer a template (search_templates → create_design with template_id) for common formats; then replace its text (replace_text) and images.
- Give each page one clear focal point. Use size and weight for hierarchy (for example a 1080 px square post: headline 72–110 px, body 28–36 px).
- Keep margins generous (about 5–8% of the page width) and keep spacing consistent; align edges to each other.
- Use at most two font families. Keep strong contrast between text and its background (dark on light or light on dark; check with a screenshot).
- Presentations: one idea per slide, short text, large visuals; put the full script in speaker notes.
- Fix every problem edit_design reports in its check field, then judge the overall look with screenshot_page.
"#;

pub fn instructions(brand: &str) -> String {
    INSTRUCTIONS.replace("{brand}", brand)
}

pub fn guide(brand: &str) -> String {
    GUIDE.replace("{brand}", brand).replace("{shapes}", &oc_schema::elements::SHAPE_KINDS.join(", ")).replace("{charts}", &oc_schema::elements::CHART_KINDS.join(", "))
}
